## Try it on your PC

No hardware needed: [`examples/host`](examples/host/README.md) runs the driver against a simulated part.

```bash
cmake -S examples/host -B build-example && cmake --build build-example && ./build-example/xmpl100_host_example
```

## Use it on your MCU

**1. Wire it** ([Hardware](docs/hardware.md) has every pin, the transfer function and the power):

| MCU | XMPL100 | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ADC input | VOUT | An input range that covers 0 V to 1.8 V |
| 3.3 V | VDD | 100 nF to GND close to the pin |
| GND | GND | Same ground as the ADC reference |

**2. Write the port**: a class with `EnsureInitialized()` (bring the ADC up), `ReadVoltageMv()` (one calibrated
conversion) and `DelayUs()`, about 20 lines on most SDKs. [Porting](docs/porting.md) has the contract and a
complete example.

**3. Read the temperature:**

<!-- generated: quickstart-code -->
<!-- /generated -->

`EnsureInitialized()` is the only call that starts the sensor. Every other call returns `Error::NotInitialized`
until it has succeeded, and no read ever starts the sensor on its own.

## What it does

- Powers the sensor (SHDN_N, when your port drives it), waits t_ON and checks the output is in range.
- Reads the temperature through your MCU's ADC, averaging several conversions.
- Converts with the datasheet transfer function, or a calibrated offset and slope.
- Returns `Result<T>` with an `Error` from every call: no exceptions, no heap, no RTTI, no virtual calls.

## Install

```bash
git submodule add https://github.com/kokeb-labs/driver-template.git external/driver-template
```

```cmake
add_subdirectory(external/driver-template)
target_link_libraries(my_app PRIVATE xmpl100::xmpl100)
```

Plain CMake covers any MCU (STM32, RP2040, nRF, ...) and the host.
